## ***********************************************************************
## **
## **  APPSYS.INC.BL -- start an application politely, and leave it the
## **  way you found it.
## **
## **      APPSYS.STARTUP     remember the screen the user had
## **      APPSYS.RESTORE     put it back, on the way out
## **      APPSYS.ISEMU       am I running on the emulator?
## **
## **  Usage:
## **      GOSUB APPSYS.STARTUP
## **      ... the application, laid out with APPSYS.COLS / APPSYS.ROWS
## **      GOSUB APPSYS.RESTORE : END
## **
## **  A screen rectangle through a file is STASH.FILE.SAVE / .LOAD / .PUT in
## **  STASHFILE.INC.BL. Draining the keyboard is KB.CLEARKB in KB.INC.BL.
## **
## ***********************************************************************
#IFNDEF APPSYS.DEFS
#DEFINE APPSYS.DEFS 1

## The KERNAL's screen_mode. Carry set reports -- mode in .A, width .X, height .Y;
## carry clear sets. GPC's own SCREEN is the carry-clear half of this call.
#DEFINE APPSYS.SCRMODE $FF5F

## The current text colour, packed background * 16 + foreground -- the packing the GP
## drawing commands take, and the byte they read when their colour is left off.
#DEFINE APPSYS.COLREG $0376

## screen_set_charset, and the byte holding the charset number in its low bits with
## flags in the high, bit 6 being ISO.
##
## WARNING: $FF62 only sets, and does not clear bit 6. Restoring the charset alone
## leaves a machine that believes it is in ISO mode, so the whole byte goes back.
#DEFINE APPSYS.SETCHR $FF62
#DEFINE APPSYS.CHRREG $0372

## The emulator's I/O registers. The last two answer 49 and 54 -- "1" and "6" -- and
## that pair is the whole of the detection. $9FB8 moves and $9FBC is not a register.
#DEFINE APPSYS.EMUSIG $9FBE

GOTO APPSYS.SKIP

## APPSYS.STARTUP -- remember what the user had, and report the size.
##
##   out  APPSYS.MODE    the screen mode, for APPSYS.RESTORE
##        APPSYS.CHARSET the charset number, likewise
##        APPSYS.COLS    columns, e.g. 80
##        APPSYS.ROWS    rows, e.g. 60
##        APPSYS.COLOUR  the packed text colour
##
## Call it before any SCREEN or COLOR of your own: it restores the state it finds.
## Lay out from APPSYS.COLS and APPSYS.ROWS rather than 80x60 -- SCREEN 0 is 40x30.

APPSYS.STARTUP:  GP.CALL APPSYS.SCRMODE, 0, 0, 0, 1 : APPSYS.MODE = GP.A : APPSYS.COLS = GP.X : APPSYS.ROWS = GP.Y
    APPSYS.COLOUR = PEEK(APPSYS.COLREG) : APPSYS.CHARSET = PEEK(APPSYS.CHRREG)
    RETURN

## APPSYS.RESTORE -- the screen as it was, for whatever runs next.
##
## Setting the mode clears the screen. Restoring the colour matters as much: leave it
## and every later PRINT, BASIC's own included, comes out in what the application set.
##
## AND 15 is safe only because a packed attribute is 0..255. AND is 16-bit signed here
## and raises OUT OF RANGE above 32767.

APPSYS.RESTORE:  GP.CALL APPSYS.SCRMODE, APPSYS.MODE, 0, 0, 0 : GP.CALL APPSYS.SETCHR, APPSYS.CHARSET AND 63, 0, 0, 0
    POKE APPSYS.CHRREG, APPSYS.CHARSET : COLOR APPSYS.COLOUR AND 15, INT(APPSYS.COLOUR / 16)
    RETURN

## APPSYS.ISEMU -- x16emu, or the machine?
##
##   out  APPSYS.IS.EMULATOR   -1 under x16emu, 0 otherwise
##
## It answers for x16emu and nothing else: a 0 is a real machine or another emulator.
## $9FA0-$9FBF is expansion card I/O, so a card at I/O5 could answer too. Treat a 1 as
## strong and a 0 as certain.
##
## -1 and not 1, so that NOT works on it: NOT is -x-1 here, so NOT -1 is 0 while
## NOT 1 is -2, which is still true.
##
## WARNING: the RETURN is on its own line and has to be. A false IF skips the whole
## line, so a RETURN written after the test is reached only on the emulator; off it
## the routine falls out of itself into whatever follows and dies on a full frame
## stack, as OUT OF MEMORY @ $0077.

APPSYS.ISEMU:  APPSYS.IS.EMULATOR = 0 : IF PEEK(APPSYS.EMUSIG) = 49 THEN IF PEEK(APPSYS.EMUSIG + 1) = 54 THEN APPSYS.IS.EMULATOR = -1
    RETURN

APPSYS.SKIP:
#ENDIF
